Microsoft 365 Copilot
Cross-Department Playbook
Use cases, prompts, and techniques for daily work across the firm



A practical reference for every team

How to use this playbook
This document is organised in three parts. Part 1 covers the Copilot apps and surfaces themselves — what each one is good for and how to use it well. Part 2 walks through every major department in the firm with concrete day-to-day use cases and ready-to-paste prompts. Part 3 covers prompting techniques, governance reminders, and a suggested rollout approach.
Skim it once front-to-back, then come back to your department section as a daily reference. Every prompt below is a starting point — change names, dates, file references, and tone to match your situation.

Part 1 — Copilot across the M365 surfaces
Copilot in Word
Strongest for first drafts, rewrites, summarisation, comparison of document versions, and structured generation from existing source files. Always reference your own files (use the / shortcut) so output matches your firm's voice and policies rather than generic Copilot defaults.
Universal use cases
	•	First drafts of policies, memos, proposals, briefs, reports, and standard operating procedures
	•	Rewriting for tone, audience, or reading level (executive summary vs. plain English vs. legal)
	•	Summarising long documents into 1-page briefs or executive summaries
	•	Comparing two document versions and listing every substantive change
	•	Generating tables of contents, glossaries, FAQs, and appendices from existing content
	•	Translating documents while preserving formatting and tone
Prompts that work well
	•	Summarise this 30-page document into a one-page brief for senior leadership. Lead with the recommendation, then 3 supporting points, then risks.
	•	Rewrite this section in plain English at an 8th-grade reading level. Keep all technical and legal requirements intact.
	•	Compare /Policy_v1.docx and /Policy_v2.docx and list every substantive change in a table with columns: Section, Old Wording, New Wording, Likely Reason.
	•	Draft a 2-page client memo on [topic] using the structure of /Standard_Memo_Template.docx and the data in /Q3_Analysis.xlsx.
Tip: Word now offers a Claude model option alongside the OpenAI models. For long-form drafting and nuanced rewrites, Claude often produces noticeably better prose; switch via the model picker in the Copilot pane.
Copilot in Excel
Best for analysis, formula generation, data cleaning, chart creation, and Q&A over structured data. Excel Copilot dramatically improves when your data is in a proper Table (Ctrl+T) with clean headers and no merged cells.
Universal use cases
	•	Cleaning messy exports — deduplication, date format standardisation, fixing inconsistent text
	•	Building formulas in plain English ("calculate annualised growth between 2022 and 2025")
	•	Generating PivotTables, charts, and conditional formatting on demand
	•	Variance analysis, trend detection, outlier flagging
	•	Categorising open-text fields (survey responses, ticket descriptions, exit interviews) into themes
	•	Forecasting and scenario modelling using historical data
Prompts that work well
	•	Add a column showing month-over-month growth as a percentage, and conditionally format negatives in red.
	•	Categorise the comments in column C into 5–7 themes and add a column with the assigned theme.
	•	Build a PivotTable showing total revenue by region and product line, then create a bar chart from it.
	•	Find any rows where the date in column B is more than 60 days after the date in column A and highlight them.
	•	Forecast next quarter's values for column D using the last 24 months of data, and show the 80% confidence interval.
Copilot in PowerPoint
Best when you give it structured input — a Word document, a brief, or even a webpage URL — rather than a blank canvas. Copilot in PowerPoint can now also use public webpages as source material when building decks.
Universal use cases
	•	Turning a Word document or report into a 10–15 slide presentation with speaker notes
	•	Creating internal training decks, onboarding decks, and process explainers
	•	Restyling an existing deck to match the firm template
	•	Generating speaker notes for slides that don't have them
	•	Translating decks to other languages while keeping layouts intact
	•	Summarising a long deck into an executive 5-slider
Prompts that work well
	•	Create a 12-slide deck from /ProjectReport.docx. Audience: executive leadership. Include a title slide, agenda, 3 highlights, 3 risks, recommended next steps, and an appendix slide for detailed numbers.
	•	Reformat this deck using our company template and modernise the visual style. Keep all content unchanged.
	•	Add concise speaker notes (3–5 bullets per slide) for the entire presentation.
	•	Build a deck explaining [topic] using https://[reference-url] as source material.
Copilot in Outlook
Among the highest-frequency, highest-ROI surfaces — most professionals lose hours a day to email triage. Use it for catching up, drafting, and tone-checking.
Universal use cases
	•	"Catch me up" on a long email thread or after time away
	•	Drafting replies in your typical voice, then refining tone (more direct, more diplomatic, shorter)
	•	Suggesting follow-ups based on outstanding requests in your inbox
	•	Drafting status updates by pulling from recent emails and meetings
	•	Generating audio overviews of long attached files for listening on commutes
Prompts that work well
	•	Summarise this thread in 5 bullets. Highlight any open questions or commitments I made.
	•	Draft a polite reply declining this request. Suggest [alternative] and offer to help next quarter.
	•	Catch me up on everything from [client name] over the last 2 weeks across email and Teams.
	•	Draft an email asking for a 1-week extension on the [project] deliverable. Professional, brief, no excuses.
Copilot in Teams (chats and meetings)
Two distinct experiences — meeting Copilot during/after calls, and chat Copilot for ongoing conversations and channels.
Universal use cases
	•	Real-time recap during a meeting ("what did I miss in the last 10 minutes")
	•	Post-meeting: action items, decisions, unresolved questions, sentiment of attendees
	•	Catch up on a busy channel after vacation or a focus block
	•	Drafting follow-up emails or status updates from meeting transcripts
	•	Comparing what was said in two different meetings on the same topic
Prompts that work well
	•	From this meeting, list every action item with the owner, due date if mentioned, and which agenda item it relates to.
	•	Summarise the discussion on [topic]. Note any disagreements between participants and how they were resolved.
	•	Catch me up on this channel for the last 7 days. Group by topic and flag anything where my name was mentioned.
	•	Draft a follow-up email to the attendees summarising what we agreed and what we still need to decide.
Copilot in SharePoint
Two big capabilities here, both underused. First, page authoring — Copilot can draft entire intranet pages, news posts, or knowledge base articles. Second, custom agents grounded on a specific site or library, which let employees ask questions and get answers cited back to source documents.
Note: your admin has restricted agent creation, so the agent capability won't apply for your firm — but the page authoring features remain fully available.
Universal use cases
	•	Drafting intranet pages, news posts, and announcements directly from a brief
	•	Generating FAQ sections, project landing pages, and team site content
	•	Restructuring messy SharePoint sites — Copilot can suggest navigation, taxonomies, and content groupings
	•	Translating SharePoint content for multilingual audiences
Prompts that work well
	•	Create a SharePoint page announcing our new [policy/benefit/initiative]. Include a 1-paragraph overview, 3 key changes, an FAQ with 5 questions, and a contact section.
	•	Draft a project landing page for [project]. Include purpose, team members, key milestones, link section for documents, and a status block.
Copilot Notebooks
A persistent workspace where you pin a curated set of files, links, and notes as a knowledge base, then chat with Copilot grounded only on those sources. Notebooks support long prompts (around 18,000 characters versus 2,000 in standard chat) and can generate Word docs, PowerPoint decks, mind maps, and audio overviews from their contents.
When to choose Notebook over standard Chat
	•	Long-running projects (proposals, tenders, board prep, audits, investigations)
	•	Tasks that span many source files and need consistent grounding across sessions
	•	Cross-document analysis — "what's inconsistent across these 12 policies?"
	•	Onboarding workspaces — pin all the materials a new joiner needs
	•	Knowledge synthesis — research notes, interview transcripts, market scans
Audio Overviews (the "podcast" feature)
Notebooks can generate a podcast-style audio discussion between two AI hosts based on your pinned content. You can choose one or two speakers, adjust tone (concise to playful), and pick length (short, medium, long). Use cases:
	•	Turn a 50-page benefits guide into a 12-minute podcast for new hires
	•	Make policy updates digestible — leaders listen on their commute
	•	Create a "this month at the firm" audio recap from a notebook of internal updates
	•	Project retrospective podcast for teams to listen to at the end of an engagement
	•	Audio briefings for board members ahead of meetings
Prompts that work well in a Notebook
	•	Across all the documents in this notebook, identify any inconsistencies, contradictions, or gaps. Group findings by severity.
	•	Generate a Word document summarising the key arguments in these 8 research papers, with a comparison table of methodologies and conclusions.
	•	Create a PowerPoint deck pitching the recommendation in [pinned brief], using supporting evidence from the other files in this notebook.
	•	Generate an audio overview, conversational tone, two hosts, around 10 minutes, focused on the practical implications for managers.
Important: Notebook sharing currently works only inside your own M365 tenant — you cannot share notebooks externally. Plan accordingly when working with external collaborators.
Researcher (deep reasoning agent)
Researcher is Copilot's deep reasoning capability — it performs multi-step research combining your work data with the public web, taking longer but producing far more thorough analysis than a single chat turn. Use it when the question genuinely needs synthesis across many sources.
Good fits for Researcher
	•	Competitive analysis combining internal documents and external research
	•	Market entry studies, vendor evaluations, build-vs-buy analyses
	•	Regulatory landscape reviews — what's changing, who's affected, what to do about it
	•	Strategic option papers requiring evidence from many angles
	•	Best-practice benchmarking against the firm's own current state
Prompts that work well
	•	Research the current state of [market/topic]. Synthesise the 3 most credible perspectives, then assess how our internal approach (per /InternalStrategy.docx) compares. End with 5 specific recommendations.
	•	Build a vendor comparison for [category] covering the top 5 providers. For each: pricing model, integration profile, security posture, customer reviews, recent product changes. Recommend a shortlist of 2.
	•	Analyse our last 4 quarters of [data type] and compare patterns to industry benchmarks. Identify the top 3 areas where we're underperforming and propose responses.
Copilot Create (images, video, posters, surveys)
A standalone surface for generating designed content from prompts, templates, or your brand kit — images, videos, podcasts, posters, surveys, and short multimedia explainers. PowerPoint also now offers a model picker for image generation (GPT-Image, Flux, or Auto).
Universal use cases
	•	Short explainer videos (60–120 seconds) for internal launches, training, comms
	•	Onboarding welcome videos personalised by department or role
	•	Visual aids and infographics for policy rollouts and town halls
	•	Recruitment marketing — "day in the life" content
	•	Internal surveys with built-in analysis afterwards
	•	Posters and visual campaigns for wellness, DEI, or operational initiatives
Prompts that work well
	•	Create a 90-second explainer video on our new [policy/tool/process]. Friendly tone. Include text overlays for the key takeaways.
	•	Design a poster for our wellness week, professional but warm tone, 4 themes per day.
	•	Build a 6-question pulse survey on team workload and stress, with a balance of scale and open-text questions.
Work vs. Web — using both modes well
Copilot Chat has two grounding modes that fundamentally change what it can answer.
Work mode
Grounds responses in your tenant — your emails, Teams, SharePoint, OneDrive, calendar. Use for any question that depends on internal context.
	•	"Catch me up on what I missed last week"
	•	"What did we decide in the [project] meetings last quarter?"
	•	"Find every document related to [topic] and summarise its current status"
	•	"Who are the experts in the firm on [subject], based on documents and meetings?"
Web mode
Grounds responses in the public internet. Use for benchmarks, trends, regulations, vendor research, current events.
	•	"What's the current state of [regulation] in [jurisdiction]?"
	•	"Compare the top 5 [products] in [category] for an enterprise of our size"
	•	"Recent best practices for [process]"
Combined power prompts
	•	Compare our [policy/process] (per /OurPolicy.docx) to current industry standards in [region]. Highlight every gap.
	•	Based on our [internal data] and recent market data, is our pricing for [product] competitive? Recommend adjustments.

