Part 3 — Prompting techniques and adoption
Prompting frameworks that consistently outperform
The single biggest predictor of how much value someone gets from Copilot is prompt quality. Two simple frameworks cover most cases.
The GCSE framework — Goal, Context, Source, Expectation
	•	Goal: what are you trying to produce or decide?
	•	Context: who is it for, what's the situation, what's the constraint?
	•	Source: which files, emails, meetings, or web sources should Copilot use?
	•	Expectation: format, length, tone, and quality criteria
Example: "Draft a termination letter (Goal). Performance-based, employee on PIP for 90 days, in California (Context). Use /TerminationTemplate.docx and /CompanyTone.docx (Source). One page, legally compliant, empathetic, with appeal rights section (Expectation)."
The RISE framework — Role, Intent, Scope, Expectations
	•	Role: what perspective should Copilot adopt? ("Act as a CFO reviewing this proposal")
	•	Intent: exactly what you want produced
	•	Scope: boundaries — sources, timeframe, audience, format
	•	Expectations: quality criteria, length, tone, what to avoid
Techniques that meaningfully improve output
	•	Reference your files. Type / in the Copilot pane to attach specific documents, emails, meetings, or people. Grounded prompts beat generic ones every time.
	•	Iterate, don't restart. Refine with follow-ups: "shorter," "more empathetic," "add a section on X," "match the tone of /OtherDoc.docx."
	•	Persona prompting. "Act as a senior employment lawyer reviewing this for risk" or "Act as a CFO sceptical of this business case." Personas dramatically change the lens.
	•	Show, don't tell. Paste an example of the style or format you want, then say "match this style."
	•	Ask for structure. "Give me this as a table with columns for Issue, Risk, Recommendation, Owner." Structured outputs are easier to act on.
	•	Red-team prompts. "What's missing? What's the weakest argument? How might this be misinterpreted? What edge cases haven't I addressed?"
	•	Prompt chaining. Break complex requests into steps. Ask Copilot to summarise highlights first, then build the deck from the summary, then refine speaker notes — better than one big request.
	•	Positive direction. "Write in a conversational tone" works better than "don't be too formal." Tell Copilot what you want, not what to avoid.
	•	Specify the audience. "For executives who skim" vs. "for technical reviewers who want detail" produces dramatically different outputs.
Common mistakes to avoid
	•	Vague prompts ("summarise this") — always specify length, structure, and audience
	•	Asking Copilot to do too much in one prompt — chain instead
	•	Not referencing files — generic prompts produce generic output
	•	Trusting numbers without verification — always sanity-check financial or factual claims
	•	Pasting confidential data into web mode — always use Work mode for internal content
	•	Forgetting that Copilot inherits your permissions — it can only see what you can see
Governance and safe use
Copilot inherits the user's existing M365 permissions, sensitivity labels, and retention policies — it cannot grant access to anything you can't already see. That said, a few practical reminders for the firm:
	•	Sensitivity labels matter — DLP policies for Copilot will block summarisation of highly confidential files. Don't try to work around this.
	•	Always verify factual outputs — Copilot can hallucinate dates, numbers, and citations
	•	For client-facing or legal-bearing output, treat Copilot drafts as first drafts requiring human review
	•	When in doubt about whether a prompt is appropriate, ask whether you'd be comfortable with the output appearing in a discovery request
Suggested rollout sequence for any team
Rather than dumping everything on people at once, sequence adoption by complexity and time-to-value:
	•	Week 1: Outlook (catch-up, drafting) and Word (rewrites, summarisation). Highest immediate relief, lowest learning curve.
	•	Week 2: Teams (meeting recaps, action items) and Excel (formula help, basic analysis on Tables).
	•	Week 3: Notebooks for ongoing projects, and SharePoint page authoring.
	•	Week 4 and beyond: Researcher for deeper analyses, Create for visual content, advanced Excel scenarios.
Pair each stage with 5–10 starter prompts specific to that team's actual work. Generic training lands far worse than role-specific examples. Designate "Copilot champions" per department who collect prompts that work and share them in a central library — your firm's prompt library will be more valuable than any external resource within a few months.
Building a firm-wide prompt library
As people develop prompts that work, capture them in a shared SharePoint library or OneNote notebook. Organise by:
	•	Application (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, Teams, Notebooks)
	•	Department (HR, Finance, Sales, Legal, etc.)
	•	Use case (drafting, summarising, analysis, comparison, brainstorming)
For each prompt, record: the prompt itself, the typical input, what good output looks like, and any tweaks that improve results. Within a quarter, this becomes one of the most valuable internal assets the firm has.

Final thoughts
Copilot's value compounds. The first week feels marginal, the first month is noticeably faster, and by the third month most users would push back hard if you took the licenses away. The teams that get there fastest do three things: they reference files in every prompt, they iterate rather than restart, and they share what works.
Treat this playbook as a starting point. The most valuable prompts in your firm three months from now will be ones nobody has written yet — discovered by your own people doing their own work.
